.TH LT-QWWAD_TX_SINGLE_BARRIER "1" "September 2016" "lt-qwwad_tx_single_barrier (Quantum Wells, Wires and Dots) 1.5.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lt-qwwad_tx_single_barrier \- compute transmission coefficient through single tunnelling barrier.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lt-qwwad_tx_single_barrier
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Find the transmission coefficient through a single tunnelling barrier.
.SH OPTIONS

Generic options:
.TP
\fB\-h\fR [ \fB\-\-help\fR ]
display a help message
.TP
\fB\-v\fR [ \fB\-\-version\fR ]
display the version of the program
.HP
\fB\-c\fR [ \fB\-\-config\fR ] arg (=qwwad.cfg) name of the configuration file to be used
.SS "Program-specific options:"
.HP
\fB\-L\fR [ \fB\-\-barrierwidth\fR ] arg (=100) Width of barrier [angstrom].
.TP
\fB\-m\fR [ \fB\-\-mass\fR ] arg (=0.067)
Effective mass (relative to that of a free
electron). This is assumed to be constant
throughout the whole system.
.TP
\fB\-\-barrierpotential\fR arg (=100)
Barrier potential [meV]
.TP
\fB\-d\fR [ \fB\-\-dE\fR ] arg (=0.1)
Energy step [meV]
.SS "Configuration options:"
.TP
\fB\-V\fR [ \fB\-\-verbose\fR ]
display lots of information about calculation
.SH FILES
.SS Output files:
  'T.r'    Transmission coefficient as a function of energy:
           Column 1: energy of incident carrier [meV].
           Column 2: transmission coefficient.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compute the transmission coefficient through a 100-meV barrier, with 200-angstrom width:
   qwwad_tx_single_barrier --barrierwidth 200 --barrierpotential 100

Compute the transmission coefficient through a 1-eV barrier, with default width and effective mass = 0.1 m0 using resolution of 1 meV:
   qwwad_tx_single_barrier --barrierpotential 1000 --mass 0.1 --dE 1
